INT. CURLING CLUB - SATURDAY MORNING

Empty sheet of ice. Rocks lined up like sleeping animals. A scoreboard that says nothing.

GRETA HOLMQUIST (40s, skip, club jacket) sweeps a rock into place. BJORN LUND (50s, vice, thermos) watches from the hack. ILSE NAKAMURA (20s, newest member, brand-new broom) stands too straight.

A STRANGER (30s, rental shoes, borrowed gloves) pushes open the glass door and stands at the edge of the ice.

GRETA
Closed to the public.

STRANGER
I called. A woman said Saturday.

BJORN
That was Ilse.

ILSE
I said come at noon.

STRANGER
It's eleven fifty.

Bjorn laughs into his thermos. Greta says nothing. She slides a rock back to the hack.

GRETA
What's your name?

STRANGER
Felix.

GRETA
Felix. Ever held a broom?

FELIX
My father curled. I never did.

GRETA
That's the best kind.

She hands him her broom. Felix steps onto the ice, arms out for balance. Ilse shifts to make room. Bjorn lifts the thermos, pours a fourth cup, and sets it on the boards.
